==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from the {{bor|en|de|Coitus interruptus}}, a [[Latinism]] of {{m|de|Coitus|pos=the then standard spelling of {{m|de|Koitus}}}} and {{m|la|interruptus|pos=Latin for “interrupted”}} in 1886, a year after its German coining. Its antonym, {{m|en|coitus reservatus}}, was borrowed four years later, in 1890, despite having been coined five years before it, in 1880, in German.<ref name="etym">{{R:Etymonline|coitus|31. July, 2026}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌkəʊ̯.ɪtəs ˌɪntəˈrʌptəs/|/ˌkɔɪ̯.təs-/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌkɔɪ̯.təs ˌɪntəˈrʌptəs/|/ˌkoʊ̯.ɪtəs-/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-coitus interruptus.wav|a=US|IPA=/ˌkoʊ.ɪ-/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|nolink=1}}

# [[sexual intercourse]] [[interrupted]] by [[withdrawal]] of the [[penis]] before [[ejaculation]] {{defdate|from 1886<ref name="etym"></ref>}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|coitus reservatus}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author={{w|Colin Jones (historian)|Colin Jones}}|title=The Great Nation|page=351|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2003
|passage=Yet in the event, population continued to grow – even though signs began to appear that '''coitus interruptus''' was beginning to be used systematically to reduce family size.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|url=http://www.theguardian.com/society/2003/oct/07/lifeandhealth.medicineandhealth|title=Men on the pill? Yeah, right|date=2003-10-07|author=Lucy Atkins|passage=We have come some way since sheep's bladder condoms but male contraceptive options are still pretty basic. Not counting one Chinese invention - a small electronic device worn in the underpants, which causes infertility for a month after a current is switched on briefly - it is still down to the old favourites: abstinence, '''coitus interruptus''', condoms or vasectomy.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261020135924/https://www.theguardian.com/society/2003/oct/07/lifeandhealth.medicineandhealth|archivedate=20 October 2026}}

